Z||000002||Butter||12-10-2001||05:08 PM||||Sounds like the plastic gear has shelled some teeth.  Go Hear <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html</A> <BR>He can help.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000003||Rumor||12-10-2001||05:32 PM||||How does the system know when to stop turning the motor? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||stingant0||12-10-2001||05:59 PM||||I know what happend, it was those darn fiero gremlins!! I hate those things. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||12-10-2001||08:49 PM||||The 84-86 headlight motors are all the same.<BR>They are designed to run until the headlight mechanism hits a stop.  When the stop is hit, the armature shaft will move against a spring as the motor stalls out.  This opens up a set of contacts and opens the circuit to the motor and shuts off the current.  The biggest drawback to this design is that the motor gears are always in a bind with a fair amount of load.  Over time the plastic teeth on the large gear get brittle and tend to break.  When this happens the motor can't develope enough torque to open the contacts and it will keep spinning.  The best solution is a new set of gears and rubber cushions from Rodney Dickman.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000006||NEO Cesek Racing||12-10-2001||10:58 PM||||i have the same problem. i posted on here and got plenty of info (BTW thanks everyone). i bought a used replacement motor but i wound up getting one that also didn't work. after much cursing and gnashing of teeth i tore apart my extra headlight motor and found that the plastic bumpers were disintigrated. i just have to order the rebuild kit from rodney but i'm a poor mofo.<BR>the only question i still have is how to get the plastic gear off the shaft so i can put the new gear and bumpers in. ||||reg||1
